# Objective and Context

"Write a plan to set up scaffolding for the prototype. The goal is to get to the point where you have an input box, the user can type in a message, and the agent can stream a response."

Per `notes/prototype.md`: an agentic harness where the user types, the agent responds and (eventually) uses tools. Everything is client-side; the backend's entire job is to hold the API key and proxy one streaming call. The inference manager, the message array, and the agent loop all live in the browser.

Scaffolding is ripped from two existing repos:

- `~/src/magenta.nvim/node/core/src/providers/` - the provider/inference abstraction. We borrow the *type vocabulary*, not the code. Nothing from `thread.ts`, `thread-api.ts`, `thread-supervisor.ts`, or `agent.ts` (which imports both) may be depended on.
- `~/src/gatherus/main/` - client/server split, vite, vitest, vamp.

Key entities we are mirroring (exact shapes at `magenta.nvim/node/core/src/providers/provider-types.ts`):

- `StreamingBlock` - `{type:"text",text} | {type:"thinking",thinking} | {type:"tool_use",id,name,inputJson}`. The unit of streaming progress.
- `RequestUpdate` - `{type:"streaming-block",streamingBlock} | {type:"block-finished"} | {type:"retry-scheduled",retry} | {type:"attempt-started"}`. This is what crosses the wire.
- `InferenceRequest` - `{promise: Promise<RequestResult>, abort(): void}`.
- `RequestResult` - `{type:"tool_use",requested} | {type:"stopped",stopReason} | {type:"aborted"} | {type:"error",error}`.
- `NativeInferenceManager` - owns the native `Anthropic.MessageParam[]` array; `appendUserMessage`, `appendToolResults`, `sendRequest(onUpdate)`.
- `AgentInput` - text/image/document content the user submits. We start with text only.

Magenta's `AnthropicInferenceManager` is 1240 lines (retry, OAuth/keychain auth, cache control, token counting, conversion caching). We do not port it; we write ~150 lines with the same interface shape, so the real thing can be swapped in later.

Relevant files (all new):

- `package.json`, `vite.config.ts`, `biome.json`, `tsconfig.json` - root workspace, modeled on gatherus.
- `packages/iso/protocol.ts` - the client<->server websocket message union.
- `packages/iso/streaming.ts` - `StreamingBlock`, `RequestUpdate`, `StopReason`. Shared because the server produces them and the client consumes them.
- `packages/backend/app.ts` - fastify entry, listens on 3000.
- `packages/backend/create-app.ts` - app factory (no listen), so tests can drive it.
- `packages/backend/socket.ts` - `GET /api/socket`. Owns an `Anthropic` client and nothing else.
- `packages/backend/stream.ts` - runs one `messages.stream()` call, translating SDK events into `RequestUpdate`.
- `packages/frontend/inference.ts` - the inference manager: owns the message array, calls the socket.
- `packages/frontend/agent-loop.ts` - the turn loop.
- `packages/frontend/socket-client.ts` - request/response-with-stream over one socket.
- `packages/frontend/{index.html,main.ts,vamp.ts,transcript.ts,composer.ts}`.

# Design

The backend is stateless. It holds no conversation, no message array, no session. A `start-request` frame carries the *complete* `Anthropic.MessageCreateParams` - model, system prompt, tools, and the full message array - and the server does `client.messages.stream(params)`, forwarding each `RequestUpdate` back down the socket tagged with the request id, then a terminal frame. This is a pure function of its input, which is what makes it testable and what keeps every interesting decision in the browser where the prototype's UI experiments need it.

Consequences worth stating, since they cut against the usual instinct:

- The whole conversation is re-uploaded on every turn. At prototype scale this is free, and it buys us a server with no lifecycle.
- Refresh discards everything, which `notes/prototype.md` asks for and which now falls out of the architecture rather than needing enforcement.
- Prototype 1 (highlight transcript text, open a side panel, fork a sub-conversation) is a purely client-side change. So is prototype 2's "suggest review points" - it is just another `start-request` with different params. That is the payoff for putting the manager in the browser.

Data flow for one turn:

composer -> `dispatch({type:"send",text})` -> `runAgentLoop` -> `manager.appendUserMessage` -> `manager.sendRequest(onUpdate)` -> `socketClient.stream(params, onUpdate)` -> `start-request` frame -> server `runStream()` -> SDK events -> `update` frames -> client `onUpdate(RequestUpdate)` -> reducer folds into the tail assistant message -> `mainView.sync(state)`.

The manager accumulates blocks from the same `RequestUpdate` stream the UI sees, and on `finished` commits the assembled assistant message into its `Anthropic.MessageParam[]`. So the transcript and the message array are built from one source, and cannot drift.

Why websocket rather than a POST returning SSE: prototypes 1-3 all want several concurrent streams (main transcript plus a side-panel conversation), and multiplexing them by request id over one socket is simpler than managing a pool of fetch streams. Gatherus already has the `@fastify/websocket` pattern to copy from `packages/backend/sheets/socket.ts`.

## Interfaces

`packages/iso/streaming.ts` - lifted from magenta, minus the retry variants:

```ts
export type StreamingBlock =
  | { type: "text"; text: string }
  | { type: "thinking"; thinking: string }
  | { type: "tool_use"; id: string; name: string; inputJson: string };

export type BlockDelta =
  | { type: "text"; text: string }
  | { type: "thinking"; thinking: string }
  | { type: "input_json"; partialJson: string };

export type RequestUpdate =
  | { type: "block-start"; index: number; block: StreamingBlock }
  | { type: "block-delta"; index: number; delta: BlockDelta }
  | { type: "block-finished"; index: number };

export type StopReason = "end_turn" | "max_tokens" | "stop_sequence" | "tool_use";
export type Usage = { inputTokens: number; outputTokens: number };
```

Magenta re-emits the whole accumulated block on each delta; we send the delta, so the wire carries O(response) rather than O(response^2). `index` is the SDK's content block index, which lets the client fold updates without assuming they are ordered.

`packages/iso/protocol.ts`:

```ts
export type RequestId = string & { __requestId: true };

export type ClientMessage =
  | { type: "start-request"; requestId: RequestId; params: StreamParams }
  | { type: "abort-request"; requestId: RequestId };

export type StreamParams = {
  model: string;
  system: string;
  max_tokens: number;
  tools: ToolSpec[];
  messages: Anthropic.MessageParam[];
};

export type ServerFrame =
  | { type: "update"; requestId: RequestId; update: RequestUpdate }
  | { type: "finished"; requestId: RequestId; stopReason: StopReason; usage: Usage }
  | { type: "aborted"; requestId: RequestId }
  | { type: "error"; requestId: RequestId; message: string };
```

`StreamParams` is a hand-written subset of `Anthropic.MessageCreateParams` rather than the SDK type itself: the server must validate what it forwards, and a narrow shape makes that a few lines. The iso package depends on `@anthropic-ai/sdk` only for `MessageParam`.

`packages/backend/stream.ts`:

```ts
export function runStream(
  client: Anthropic,
  params: StreamParams,
  onUpdate: (u: RequestUpdate) => void,
): { promise: Promise<{ type: "finished"; stopReason; usage } | { type: "aborted" }>; abort(): void };
```

`packages/frontend/inference.ts`:

```ts
export type AgentInput = { type: "text"; text: string };
export type RequestedTool = { id: string; name: string; input: unknown };

export type RequestResult =
  | { type: "tool_use"; requested: RequestedTool[] }
  | { type: "stopped"; stopReason: StopReason }
  | { type: "aborted" }
  | { type: "error"; error: Error };

export type InferenceRequest = { promise: Promise<RequestResult>; abort(): void };

export class InferenceManager {
  constructor(socket: SocketClient, options: { model; systemPrompt; tools; maxTokens });
  readonly messages: ReadonlyArray<Anthropic.MessageParam>;
  appendUserMessage(content: AgentInput[]): void;
  appendToolResults(results: ReadonlyMap<string, ToolResult>): void;
  sendRequest(onUpdate: (u: RequestUpdate) => void): InferenceRequest;
}
```

`packages/frontend/agent-loop.ts`:

```ts
export type ToolExecutor = (requests: ReadonlyArray<RequestedTool>) =>
  Promise<ReadonlyMap<string, ToolResult>>;

export function runAgentLoop(deps: {
  manager: InferenceManager;
  onUpdate: (u: RequestUpdate) => void;
  executeTools: ToolExecutor;
}, input: AgentInput[]): { promise: Promise<RequestResult>; abort(): void };
```

Tools: `tools` is threaded through and `RequestResult` can be `tool_use`, but `executeTools` is a stub returning an error result for any tool. The loop's shape stays honest without porting any tools.

Client state:

```ts
type Message = { role: "user" | "assistant"; blocks: StreamingBlock[] };
type State = { messages: Message[]; status: "idle" | "streaming"; error?: string };
```

## Invariants

- The server is stateless per request. Two `start-request` frames on one socket must not interact; the only shared thing is the `Anthropic` client.
- Every `start-request` gets exactly one terminal frame (`finished` | `aborted` | `error`), so the client can always settle its promise. A socket close settles all outstanding requests as errors.
- `block-delta` and `block-finished` for an index only arrive after `block-start` for that index.
- `sendRequest` never rejects; all failures come back as `{type:"error"}`.
- An aborted request still commits a complete assistant message to `manager.messages` (magenta uses `ABORT_MARKER_TEXT`; we append a synthetic text block), so the next turn's message array is valid for the API. An assistant message with an unanswered `tool_use` block is not.
- `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is read on the server and never crosses the wire.

# Stages

## workspace scaffolding

- Goal: `npm run dev` serves an empty page at 5173 proxying `/api/` to fastify on 3000; `npm test` and `npm run typecheck` pass.
- Work: root `package.json` with `packages/*` workspaces, `vite.config.ts` from gatherus (drop the MPA/`spaFallback` bits - single page), `biome.json`, per-package tsconfigs, `packages/iso/{protocol,streaming}.ts`, `packages/backend/{app,create-app}.ts` with `/api/health`, `packages/frontend/{index.html,main.ts}` plus `vamp.ts` copied verbatim.
- Tests:
  - A backend test builds the app via `createApp()` and asserts `/api/health` responds - proves the factory/entry split works without `listen`.

## streaming proxy

- Goal: a websocket client connects to `/api/socket`, sends `start-request` with a real message array, and receives `update` frames then `finished`.
- Work: `packages/backend/stream.ts` and `socket.ts` (heartbeat + buffered-message pattern from `gatherus/.../sheets/socket.ts`, minus all auth). `@anthropic-ai/sdk` at the version magenta uses (`^0.115.0`).
- Tests:
  - The hard part is the SDK's event shape, not our bookkeeping. Follow magenta's `providers/mock-anthropic-client.ts`: a fake client whose `messages.stream()` replays a scripted `content_block_start`/`delta`/`stop` sequence. Assert the translation into `RequestUpdate`, driving it through a real socket so the wiring is covered too.
  - Two concurrent `start-request` frames on one socket interleave without cross-talk, and aborting one leaves the other running. This is the invariant a stateless server buys us, so it should be tested directly.
  - Abort mid-stream produces `aborted` and no further frames for that id.
  - A malformed `start-request` produces an `error` frame rather than killing the socket.
  - One test against the real API, skipped unless `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` is set, to catch drift between the mock and the SDK.

## client inference loop

- Goal: from the browser console, `runAgentLoop` with "count to five" streams updates and resolves `{type:"stopped"}`, with `manager.messages` holding both turns.
- Work: `socket-client.ts` (reconnect-free; a map of requestId -> pending handlers), `inference.ts`, `agent-loop.ts`.
- Tests:
  - Frontend vitest against a fake `SocketClient` that replays frame sequences - no network, no SDK.
  - A `tool_use` result runs `executeTools` and issues a second request; assert on the resulting `manager.messages`, since the tool round-trip is where the array gets corrupted.
  - Abort mid-stream leaves a well-formed assistant message with no dangling `tool_use`.
  - Socket close mid-request settles the promise as an error.

## chat UI

- Goal: the deliverable - type in the box, hit enter, watch the response stream in.
- Work: the reducer folding `RequestUpdate` into `State`, `transcript.ts` and `composer.ts` as vamp views, wired into the single root dispatch loop in `main.ts`.
- Tests:
  - Frontend vitest over the reducer: a recorded frame sequence yields the expected `State.messages`. This is where the real logic is; the DOM binding is vamp's job and is covered by using it.
  - Manual: two turns in a row, and an abort mid-stream.
